## General (Fish ≠ Bash)

### Assignment and export

**Cause:** Fish has no `VAR=value` or `export`.

**Bad:** `FOO=bar`, `export FOO=bar`

**Good:**

```fish
set -l FOO bar           # function-local
set -gx FOO bar          # global exported (like export in other shells)
set -U tide_report_units m   # universal (user config in this project)
```

### Exit status

**Bad:** `if [ $? -eq 0 ]`, `echo $?`

**Good:** `if test $status -eq 0` immediately after the command, or `set -l st $status` right after `command ...`

### Conditionals and tests

**Bad:** `[[ -f "$f" ]]`, `[ -n "$x" ]` (external `[` is slower and non-idiomatic)

**Good:** `test -f "$f"`, `test -n "$x"`, `string length -q -- "$x"`

Combine with Fish keywords: `cmd; and test $status -eq 0; and echo ok` (Fish 3.0+ also allows `&&` / `||` in many contexts; prefer `test` + `and`/`or` in this repo for clarity).

### Arithmetic

**Bad:** `count=$((a + 1))`, `$(( ... ))`

**Good:** `set count (math $a + 1)`, `math "$x * 2"`

### Redirection

**Bad:** `&>/dev/null`, `2>&1 >/dev/null` bash-isms copied blindly

**Good:** `command cmd 2>/dev/null`, `command cmd ^/dev/null` (Fish also supports `&>` but stay consistent with `2>/dev/null` in this repo per [tide-report.mdc](tide-report.mdc))

### Variables are lists (no word splitting)

**Cause:** Every variable is a **list** of elements. Unquoted `$var` expands to multiple argv words. There is no Bash-style word splitting on spaces inside one element.

**Bad:** `set PATH "$PATH:/new"` (one wrong element), iterating `$str` as if it were a string of words

**Good:**

```fish
set -l parts (string split ":" -- $some_path_var)   # when you control a string
fish_add_path /new/dir                             # for PATH-like vars
contains -- item $my_list
```

PATH-like vars ending in `PATH` split on `:` automatically; prefer `fish_add_path` over manual colon strings.

### Command substitution and quoting

**Bad:** `` output=`cmd` ``, `${var}` (syntax error in Fish)

**Good:** `set output (cmd)`, `$var` or `"$var"` (Fish does not split on spaces inside quotes the same way Bash does; quote when you need one argument or a single string with spaces)

### Concatenating `$var` with text or `(cmd)`

**Symptom:** Tide render shows `:18` instead of `20:18`, or tests pass in CI (`TERM=dumb`) but fail in a real terminal (`TERM=xterm-256color`).

**Cause:** Fish glues `$name` to following alphanumerics as one variable name (`$sc20` → `$sc20`, not `$sc` + `20`). `$name(cmd)` is a **list index**, not concatenation with command output.

**Bad:**

```fish
set -l arrow (set_color white)$arrow_symbol(set_color $bg)
set -l text "⇞$sc20:18"
```

**Good:**

```fish
set -l c1 (set_color white)
set -l c2 (set_color $bg)
set -l arrow "$c1$arrow_symbol$c2"
set -l text "prefix$sc"suffix
```

### Scope: local, global, universal

**Bad:** Assuming undeclared variables are local; using `-g` for user-facing defaults that should persist

**Good:**

```fish
set -l tmp 0                    # function-local
set -g __session_cache 1        # session global (careful in tests — see TideReport section)
set -q var; or set -U var val   # universal default once (this project's install pattern)
set -e var                      # erase
set -q var                      # existence (variable, not function — see below)
```

### Reassigning a local inside `if` / `while`

**Cause:** `set -l name value` **inside** a block creates a **new** inner local; outer binding unchanged.

**Bad:**

```fish
set -l state none
if condition
    set -l state (compute)   # outer still none
end
```

**Good:** `set state (compute)` without `-l` on reassignment (outer was created with `set -l state`).

### `(set_color)` glued to literal text drops the text

**Symptom:** Unavailable segment is blank in tests or non-interactive Fish; `(string length (set_color red)hello)` is 0.

**Cause:** `(set_color …)text` is not reliable concatenation in Fish. Assign `set_color` to a variable first, then `"$color_var$text"`.

**Bad:** `set -l out (set_color red)"$unavailable_text"`

**Good:** `set -l c (set_color red); set -l out "$c$unavailable_text"`

### `(set_color)` before `"(..."` runs command substitution on the parenthetical

**Symptom:** Install wizard shows ghost text (`(set_color normal)`, `(city, postal code…)`, empty location prompts); `read -P` prints raw fragments.

**Cause:** `(set_color brwhite)"(city, …)"` parses as `(set_color brwhite)` plus an empty `""` string plus `(city, …)` as **command substitution**, not literal parentheses.

**Bad:** `set -l prompt (set_color brcyan)"Enter "(set_color brwhite)"(city, postal code)"(set_color normal)`

**Good:** `set -l loc_hint '(city, postal code)'`; `set -l c (set_color brwhite)`; `set -l prompt (string join '' $c_cyan 'Enter ' $c_white $loc_hint $c_norm)`

### `gh auth status` writes to stdout — redirect both streams when checking only exit status

**Symptom:** After install or on the prompt, `github.com` / `Logged in to…` lines overlap the Tide prompt; health checks appear twice if wizard and install both run them.

**Cause:** `gh auth status -h github.com 2>/dev/null` hides stderr only; auth details still print to stdout. Wizard and `_tide_report_do_install` both called `_tide_report_run_health_checks`.

**Good:** `gh auth status -h github.com >/dev/null 2>&1`; run health checks once from the install orchestrator (or `tide-report configure`), not from wizard and install.

### Do not name a parameter `argv` when using `--argument-names`

**Symptom:** GitHub prompt stuck on `…`; background `gh repo view` / spawn workers never write cache; real `gh` prints subcommand help.

**Cause:** `--argument-names timeout_sec argv` binds only **one** positional to `argv` (`repo`). In Fish 4.x, `$argv` inside functions still holds **all** positionals, so you must forward `$argv[2..-1]` (or similar), not a named `argv` parameter.

**Bad:** `function __tide_report_run_gh --argument-names timeout_sec argv` then `gh $argv`

**Good:** `function __tide_report_run_gh --argument-names timeout_sec` then `set -l gh_argv $argv[2..-1]; gh $gh_argv`

### Validate HTTP bodies before piping to `jq`

**Symptom:** Wizard or doctor prints `jq: parse error: Invalid numeric literal…` during “Retrieving location…”.

**Cause:** `ipapi.co` (and similar) may return HTTP 429/403 **plain text** on rate limits; `curl -s` still captures the body and bare `jq` on non-JSON spews errors to the terminal.

**Good:** `curl -s -f …` (fail on HTTP errors), `jq -e 'type == "object"'` gate, HTTPS fallback provider, and `jq … 2>/dev/null` on parse lines.

### `read -l` from lock timestamp file can fail silently

**Symptom:** Prompt handler or test appears stuck after calling a helper that echoes several lines; direct calls to the helper return instantly.

**Cause:** `read -l a b c (my_helper …)` when `my_helper` prints multiple lines may block waiting for stdin instead of consuming the substitution output (Fish 3.x).

**Bad:**

```fish
read -l trigger_fetch cache_valid stale (__tide_report_cache_state "$file" $now $refresh $expire)
```

**Good:** Capture as a list, then index or convert to booleans:

```fish
set -l state (__tide_report_cache_state "$file" $now $refresh $expire)
set -l trigger_fetch false
set -l cache_valid false
test "$state[1]" = true; and set trigger_fetch true
test "$state[2]" = true; and set cache_valid true
```

### Empty positional arguments are dropped

**Cause:** Fish **omits** `""` from `$argv`. Optional positional APIs break silently.

**Bad:** `foo "$a" "" "$b" "" "$c"` with `--argument-names` (mis-aligned slots)

**Good:** Sentinel this repo uses: `-` (normalize in callee), or fewer positionals / separate functions

```fish
__parse "$cache" - "$ci_cache" - "$display_state"
# Callee: test "$arg" = "-"; or not set -q arg[1]; and treat as omitted
```

### Function name must match filename (autoload)

**Bad:** `functions/my-helper.fish` containing `function my_helper`

**Good:** `functions/my_helper.fish` with `function my_helper` (Fisher/Tide autoload by name). See [fish-functions-no-top-level-execution.mdc](fish-functions-no-top-level-execution.mdc).

### Use Fish builtins, not Bash helpers

**Prefer:** `string`, `path`, `math`, `test`, `contains`, `count`, `argparse`, `command` (explicit PATH for externals)

**Bad:** `echo "$var" | grep`, `$(wc -l)`, `sed` for simple string ops Fish can do natively

**Good:** `string match -q '*pat*' -- "$var"`, `path basename "$f"`

### Dependencies and optional tools

**Good:**

```fish
if command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1
    ...
end
# or
command -q gh; and gh version
```

### `set -n` lists variable names (not a non-empty test)

**Symptom:** Preview or helper output explodes to megabytes when a function result is captured in `"$()"` .

**Cause:** `set -n name` prints variable **names** to stdout. It is not `test -n`.

**Bad:** `set -q var; and set -n var; and set fg $var`

**Good:** `set -q var; and test -n "$var"; and set fg $var`

### `rm` aliases block non-interactive cleanup

**Symptom:** Agent or script hangs on “Use trash-put instead?” when deleting temp dirs under `/tmp` or in tests.

**Cause:** User config wraps `rm` (e.g. trash-put training wheels). Fish resolves bare `rm` to the alias/function, not the binary.

**Bad:** `rm -rf "$tmp"`, `rm -rf /tmp/my-test-dir` in scripts or agent shell one-liners

**Good:** `command rm -rf "$tmp"` — same pattern as [`scripts/run_tests_isolated.fish`](scripts/run_tests_isolated.fish) and [`scripts/generate_prompt_previews.fish`](scripts/generate_prompt_previews.fish)

### Interactive-only features

**Cause:** Abbreviations and some `read` UX do nothing in non-interactive Fisher install / CI.

**Good:** `if status is-interactive` before wizard `read`; never rely on abbreviations in `functions/` or `scripts/`.

### Syntax check does not catch semantics

Run `fish -n path/to/file.fish` on every touched file ([fish-scripts-lint.mdc](fish-scripts-lint.mdc)). It will **not** catch dropped `""` args, wrong locals, or test pollution — run targeted tests.

